  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of the Interior obligated $5.4B on federal contracts in FY2026 to date. The department processed 22,164 awards with an average award size of $245K. Obligations fell 17.3% year over year. Fiscal-year trends show a peak in FY24 at $8.1B, followed by declines to $6.6B in FY25 and to $5.4B in FY26. Construction and IT services are the dominant purchase areas in FY2026 to date. Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction led categories at $696M, followed by Computer Systems Design Services at $448M, and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $441M. Top vendors skew toward large construction and IT contractors. GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. received $191M, AMES FEDERAL CONTRACTING GROUP LLC received $132M, and GUIDEHOUSE INC. received $132M. Those three vendors are followed by several construction firms including RECORD STEEL AND CONSTRUCTION, INC. and NW CONSTRUCTION, INC with $130M and $126M respectively. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did Interior contracting change from FY25 to FY26?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 17.3% year over year from FY25 to FY26. Fiscal-year obligations moved from $6.6B in FY25 to $5.4B in FY26. Award count and average award size in FY2026 to date are 22,164 awards and $245K per award."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which categories and vendors dominate FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction is the largest category at $696M, followed by Computer Systems Design Services at $448M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $441M. GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. is the top vendor at $191M, with AMES FEDERAL CONTRACTING GROUP LLC and GUIDEHOUSE INC. each at $132M."
      }
    ]
